`,s.jsx(i,{items:[{q:"What does the RPN replace, and what does it introduce?",a:"It replaces selective search: instead of 2 seconds of CPU superpixel merging, a small conv slides over the already-computed feature map and emits objectness plus a box refinement for k anchors at every position, in about 10 ms, learned jointly with the detector. What it introduces is the anchor — a fixed reference box per slot that the network only has to correct, which makes the target assignment unambiguous and the regression well conditioned."},{q:"Faster R-CNN scores over 20,000 anchors but sends 300 proposals to the head. Why is 300 enough when selective search needed 2,000?",a:"Because the 300 are ranked by a learned objectness score rather than a hand-designed grouping heuristic, so recall holds nearly flat as the count drops — whereas selective search's recall falls steeply below about 2,000. Better ranking means fewer candidates carry the same coverage, and the per-region head cost falls proportionally."}]}),`
